  core formula for libsodium 1.0.22 (`brew info --json=v2 libsodium`), i.e.
  from a second party we already trust to build the bottle. It has NOT been
  checked against upstream's own signature. Upstream signs every release with
  minisign under the Ed25519 public key

    RWQf6LRCGA9i53mlYecO4IzT51TGPpvWucNSCh1CBM0QTaLn73Y7GFO3

  (also a GPG/RSA key). **Verifying the .minisig against that key is OWED and
  NOT DONE.** It is cheap, it is the one thing PDFium cannot offer us at all
  (no release artefact to sign), and it should be the first step of the real
  vendoring. See README.md, "What is still owed", item 1.

Build configuration
-------------------
Homebrew's formula builds with `./configure --disable-silent-rules` and
nothing cryptographically interesting switched off; in particular this is NOT
a `--enable-minimal` build, so crypto_pwhash (Argon2id) is present. The
module asserts that at runtime rather than trusting it: a build where
crypto_pwhash is compiled out would fail uapp.crypto.kdf immediately rather
than silently losing the passphrase path.

NOT CONFIGURED, and it matters for mobile: `--enable-minimal` strips
crypto_pwhash, crypto_box_seal and several other symbols to shrink the binary.
The mobile build must decide explicitly whether to use it; we need
crypto_pwhash and crypto_kx, so the answer is almost certainly NO and the
size cost must be measured rather than assumed.

Licensing
---------
Verified against the actual file in the installed prefix, not against
upstream's website:

  * ./LICENSE is a byte-for-byte copy of
    /opt/homebrew/Cellar/libsodium/1.0.22/LICENSE
    sha256 508a76d186356c0dd807a670ef510964f8724557024796a2c426c6c0e19ab683
    It is the ISC licence, "Copyright (c) 2013-2026 Frank Denis
    <j at pureftpd dot org>". Short permissive form: use, copy, modify and/or
    distribute for any purpose with or without fee, provided the copyright
    notice and the permission notice appear in all copies. No copyleft, no
    source-disclosure trigger, no advertising clause, no patent grant and no
    patent retaliation clause.
  * ./AUTHORS is copied from the same prefix and is the contributor list the
    ISC notice's "copyright notice" obligation points at.
  * The bottle also ships sbom.spdx.json, whose `licenseConcluded` for the
    source package is "ISC" (`licenseDeclared` is NOASSERTION — Homebrew does
    not scan, it records the formula's `license` field). Corroborating
    evidence, not the determination; the determination is ./LICENSE above.

  There is NO static-vs-dynamic distinction anywhere in the ISC text. This is
  the material difference from FreeType's FTL (THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES §4) and
  from anything LGPL: static linking into a closed-source commercial binary
  distributed through the Apple App Store and Google Play is unambiguously
  permitted, with attribution as the only obligation.

  THE MOBILE DISTRIBUTION CONSTRAINT IS NOT A LICENCE CONSTRAINT. It is US
  export control (EAR Category 5 Part 2) plus Apple's
  ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption declaration. See
  docs/spikes/SP-9-findings.md §4 — that is the finding this vetting
  actually produced, and it is an operational/legal deliverable that does not
  currently exist anywhere in the repo.

Re-roll procedure
-----------------
1. Pick a release from https://github.com/jedisct1/libsodium/releases (or
   https://download.libsodium.org/libsodium/releases/).
2. Download the tarball AND its `.minisig`. Verify the signature against
   RWQf6LRCGA9i53mlYecO4IzT51TGPpvWucNSCh1CBM0QTaLn73Y7GFO3 with
   `minisign -V -P <key> -m <tarball>`. Record the SHA-256.
3. Update the version, the URL and the SHA-256 in this file. Re-copy LICENSE
   and AUTHORS out of the new tree and diff them: the ISC text has been
   stable since 2013, so ANY diff beyond the copyright year range is a
   licence change and must stop the roll (this is the AGG-2.4-to-2.5 failure
   mode THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES rule 3 warns about).
4. Re-read the release notes for primitive deprecations. libsodium has never
   removed a primitive, but it has changed *defaults*
   (crypto_pwhash_ALG_DEFAULT moved from Argon2i to Argon2id at 1.0.15), and
   this module pins crypto_pwhash_ALG_ARGON2ID13 explicitly rather than
   taking ALG_DEFAULT for exactly that reason.
5. Re-run the crypto suite. The published test vectors in
   tests/crypto/crypto_test.cpp (CFRG XChaCha20-Poly1305, RFC 7748 X25519)
   are the check that matters here: they are external to both libsodium and
   us, so they catch a roll that changed behaviour rather than merely
   compiling.

This procedure is written down but NOT scripted, and that is a deliberate
difference from third_party/pdfium/fetch_pdfium.sh. A fetch script for
libsodium would have to either (a) build it, which is the real mobile
problem and is out of scope here, or (b) wrap `brew install`, which would
dress a dev-host convenience up as supply-chain tooling. Step 2 above is the
part worth scripting first, and it is owed.
